# ALLOWLIST, for the same reason as the tenant image's. See that file.
#
# waku/ is NOT admitted, and that is the load-bearing line: it is what makes a
# `python -c "import waku"` in services.Dockerfile a build failure rather than
# a boundary violation nobody notices. README.md is not admitted either,
# which is why the Dockerfile passes --no-install-project.
*
.*

!hosted/
!pyproject.toml
!uv.lock
!LICENSE
!LICENSE-BRAND

# Re-excluded INSIDE the admitted trees. Last match wins, so every line in
# this block must stay BELOW the `!` admissions above; move one line up and it
# is undone by the admission that follows it, silently.
#
# Build noise first. `**/.pytest_cache/` and `**/.ruff_cache/` used to be
# named here and are gone: `**/.*` below covers both, and two rules for one
# job is how a reader learns to believe the wrong one is load-bearing.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc

# Secrets. `**/.*` replaced a list of four named shapes -- `**/.env`,
# `**/.env.*` and the two caches -- because that list was a denylist and a
# review walked through it by planting `.netrc` and `.aws`. Refusing EVERY
# dotfile inside an admitted tree is the strongest rule available here, and it
# costs nothing: `git ls-files waku skills` lists no dotfile at all, and
# `hosted/` has one, a .gitkeep that carries nothing.
#
# THIS IS NOT A CLOSED CLASS. A secret that is not dot-prefixed and does not
# end .pem or .key -- credentials.json, id_rsa, server.p12 -- still rides in
# with the tree that admits it, and always will: no pattern distinguishes a
# secret from a config file by name, and there is no allowlist version of this
# rule, because whole trees are what the lines above admit. Adding one more
# extension every time somebody thinks of one is the game this project has now
# lost five times. The limit is stated instead, and
# evals/hosted_docker/test_image.py plants both classes and pins which get
# through.
**/.*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
